      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To assess the safety and efficacy of fractional flow reserve (FFR) guided paclitaxel-coated balloon (PCB) treatment for de novo coronary artery lesions."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "There is limited data on PCB treatment for de novo lesions especially of major epicardial coronary arteries."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Sixty-six patients with 67 de novo lesions who underwent successful plain old balloon angioplasty (POBA) were included. If POBA-FFR was favorable (≥ 0.85), PCB was applied and if POBA-FFR was <0.85, stent implantation was preferred over PCB."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Forty-five lesions were treated with PCB (67.2%) and 22 lesions with stents (32.8%). Dual antiplatelet therapy duration was 6 weeks. Late luminal loss with PCB was significantly less than stent (0.05\u2009±\u20090.27 mm vs. 0.40\u2009±\u20090.54 mm, P\u2009=\u20090.022). The baseline FFR of target lesions was 0.69\u2009±\u20090.16 in PCB and 0.60\u2009±\u20090.11 in stent group (P\u2009=\u20090.015), however, the FFR at 9 months was not different between groups (0.85\u2009±\u20090.08 in PCB vs. 0.85\u2009±\u20090.05 in stent group, P\u2009=\u20090.973). At 1 year, one myocardial infarction and one target lesion revascularization related to in-stent restenosis were detected, both in the stent group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "POBA-FFR-guided PCB treatment is safe and effective for de novo coronary lesions with good anatomical and physiological patency at mid-term follow-up. © 2015 Wiley Periodicals, Inc."
          }
        ]
      },
